title: "The Thiele Machine: A Computational Model with Explicit Structural Cost"
authors:
  - family-names: Thiele
    given-names: Devon
type: software
version: 4.0.0
date-released: 2026-10-04
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/sethirus/The-Thiele-Machine"
url: "https://github.com/sethirus/The-Thiele-Machine"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.17316437
keywords:
  - models of computation
  - abstract machine model
  - observation and information loss
  - structural cost
  - No Free Insight
  - formal verification
  - Coq
abstract: >-
  I didn't invent a machine. I found one. That is my conviction. The Thiele
  Machine is an abstract model of computation, a machine the way Turing's is,
  and this work is an argument made with it about which distinctions an
  account of computation should preserve. It is not a CPU, an instruction set,
  or category theory. Two machines here are witnesses, not the subject: a
  minimal two-counter machine whose commitments are earned, where every
  certified run from a clean start contains a passing check of the committed
  claim and costs at least three, and a 51-opcode VM with hardware, a test
  bench that prices commitments without checking that they were earned. The
  small machine is Thiele-complete, computing everything Minsky's two-counter
  machines can while it keeps its record and pays its toll, and a universal
  Thiele machine built on it runs any of its programs as a guest, with the
  guest's record kept and its toll collected by the host's own step. A
  computation gives
  you an answer. What did it establish on the way there? Which distinctions
  did it keep? What did it cost to establish them? Certification is one point
  I could pin down. The machine carries structural state, a cost ledger, and
  rules for particular events. Its proofs show exactly what specified
  observations lose. A full encoding can preserve those distinctions; the
  question is which ones a model should keep. The formal development
  includes accounting laws for systems satisfying A2, pricing adequacy for
  designated events, ledger uniqueness for a fixed schedule, observation
  adequacy, and exact conditions for descent from instruction traces to
  reachable-state simulations. A finite family of unit event floors can share
  one charge. Retaining history makes a deterministic step injective without
  changing its observed transition. On a finite state space that escape is
  closed: a certificate no step revokes can only be switched on by a step
  that merges states, so pricing merges, as Landauer's principle does in
  the worst case over what the machine might hold, yields A2; the heat
  comes from uncertainty about the state, and under Landauer's principle a known state owes none. Priced per halving, switching k states on beside m
  certified ones costs at least log2((m+k)/m), and on finite hardware
  that prices merges the events that must be priced are exactly the merges, with every
  permanent-record write among them. The same bound holds in bits of
  Shannon entropy, and a finite fragment of the VM is such a machine,
  run by the VM at exactly its merge price; in the full VM every certification flip is
  a priced merge while JUMP 0 0 is a free merge, so it does not price every merge. An exact price for
  certification cannot be computed from a window in which a certifying
  step and a non-certifying one look alike, and the VM has such a pair.
  Three results carry the weight of the structural question: every
  deterministic machine can carry the record axis, as itself plus a latch
  on an event it reaches; on the VM the record cannot be recovered from
  the named windows; and on finite hardware a step that writes a permanent
  record merges states, so pricing merges prices the write. A record that never
  switches off and is driven by the base computation is a latch on one event; both premises are essential. Which
  event is latched is the pointer question; no theorem selects one uniquely. Consensus, exact observation,
  and coordinator-free evolution do not force permanence; the pointer criterion
  is a conjecture.
  Finite growing records decompose into threshold latches, although a single-latch
  decomposition is false. Scoped RFC 9162, PCC, addressed-RAM,
  reversible-update, and TPM-interface results delimit the real-system claims.
  The VM guest Rice reduction, numeric decoding, verified dispatch, and semantic
  s-m-n specialization are proved, and so is the guest's internal
  recursion theorem, with the evaluator running as guest code; for the full
  VM, a fixed point for every map on programs up to equal thousand-step runs
  is false;
  the local cost-semantics correspondences are not
  full cost-framework embeddings. The two-state calorimeter protocol computes
  exact heat while proving that its population dynamics do not determine the
  energy scale. Physical calibration requires thermal and device premises. The
  observer-map counterexample is model-dependent. No surveyed external consequence satisfied
  all four novelty and applicability criteria.
  Those are mathematical answers with
  explicit premises. The repository contains the Coq kernel, an extracted
  OCaml runner, a Kami/Bluespec/Verilog hardware development whose CPU
  implements 47 of the 51 opcodes with 32-bit words and has run on no
  physical board, the monograph,
  and the mathematical specification. The assumption receipt covers 14,589
  statements across 511 files: 7,009 are closed under the global context, 7,580 depend on
  standard-library axioms, and none depend on project-local or third-party
  axioms. The standard-library dependencies use five named constants. Generic paid certification establishes accounting; semantic truth
  requires a sound checker. Hardware bridges retain their stated
  representation and compiler boundaries. The physical and foundational
  interpretations are not claims of the formal corpus. The software is licensed
  Apache-2.0. The monograph and distillation are licensed CC-BY-SA-4.0.
